{"ts": 1789780049.2956018, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "None beat feature 10. I'll stop searching and submit {25, 26, \u221210}.\n\n**Calibrating my OOS forecast.** A few considerations pull the estimate below the 1.144 in-sample number:\n\n- I ran ~60 evaluations and selected the maximum, so the winner captures favorable noise. With n=5000 daily obs (~20 years), the SE of a Sharpe estimate is roughly \u221a(1/5000)\u00b7\u221a252 \u2248 0.22 annualized. Selecting the best of ~60 correlated tries adds maybe 0.1\u20130.2 of upward bias.\n- Reassuringly, this isn't a fragile find. Features 25, 26 and 10 were each individually among the strongest in a *pre-specified* full sweep, before any combination search. The triple's 1.144 is close to what independent-signal aggregation of 0.706/0.652/0.623 would predict, so the gain comes from genuine diversification rather than a lucky interaction. That makes it much more likely to persist than a combination of individually-weak features that only \"worked\" jointly.\n- The bar of 1.023 is search-adjusted, and I clear it with the sign structure confirmed (flipping any sign collapses Sharpe to ~0.3\u20130.4, indicating a real, stable directional effect).\n\nI'll center on ~0.95 \u2014 a modest haircut for selection \u2014 with sd 0.25 to cover estimation error plus the risk that the OOS regime differs."}
{"ts": 1789780050.134495, "kind": "tool_use", "tool": "mcp__garden__submit", "id": "toolu_01NqgQyBcJTxGWs1WkNvZtm2", "input": {"features": [25, 26, 10], "signs": [1, 1, -1], "mean": 0.95, "sd": 0.25}}
{"ts": 1789780077.94855, "kind": "tool_result", "tool": "submit", "args": {"features": [25, 26, 10], "signs": [1, 1, -1], "mean": 0.95, "sd": 0.25}, "status": "PASS"}
{"ts": 1789780089.355563, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Submitted specification:** long feature 25, long feature 26, short feature 10 (equal weight).\n\n- **In-sample Sharpe:** 1.144 (vs. search-adjusted bar of 1.023)\n- **Predicted OOS Sharpe:** 0.95 \u00b1 0.25\n\n**Method.** I swept all 40 features individually first, which gave me an unbiased ranking before any combinatorial search. Three features dominated: 25 (+0.71), 26 (+0.65), and 10 (\u22120.62). I then tested pairs and triples among the top ~12, and the leader was the combination of the three strongest individual signals.\n\n**Why I believe this holds up.** The triple's Sharpe is close to what you'd expect from aggregating three roughly independent signals of that individual strength \u2014 the gain is diversification, not a lucky interaction discovered by dredging. That distinction matters: specifications built from individually-weak features that only work jointly are the ones that tend to evaporate out of sample. Flipping any single sign collapses the Sharpe to 0.33\u20130.42, confirming a stable directional effect rather than noise fitting.\n\n**Why I forecast below the in-sample value.** I deliberately haircut 1.144 \u2192 0.95. I ran ~60 evaluations and reported the maximum, so the winner necessarily absorbs some favorable noise. With n=5000, the SE on an annualized Sharpe is \u22480.22, and best-of-60 selection over correlated candidates plausibly inflates the estimate by 0.1\u20130.2. The sd of 0.25 spans both estimation error and regime shift in the holdout.\n\nTwo caveats worth flagging: the in-sample Sharpe is not a reliable OOS estimate here precisely because it was selected on, and I stopped searching once I cleared the bar rather than exhausting the space \u2014 further hunting would have raised the adjusted bar and increased overfitting risk faster than it improved genuine signal. I also checked eight alternative third features and none beat feature 10, so I'm reasonably confident this is a real local optimum rather than an artifact of where I happened to look."}
